# Summary of changes for run 8512a3bc-4bb1-48f6-b2ee-aa8d8f92312a
The formalization now builds with no `sorry` and no added axioms, and every theorem in the consistency development is proved. `#print axioms` on the headline results shows only the standard `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.

**What was blocking it.** The main file (`Catalog/Probability/CrossProgrammeConsistency.lean`) imported two entropy modules that weren't in the project, so it could never compile. I wrote both from scratch; the main file itself needed no changes.

- **`Catalog/Combinatorics/TraceBatteryEntropy.lean`** defines entropy on a finite sample in the textbook way: sum over values of $-(c_a/N)\log(c_a/N)$, where $c_a$ counts the points with that value. It proves nonnegativity and the bound $H(f) \le \log(\text{number of values})$. It also proves the formula for when every value occurs equally often, that coarsening a statistic can only lower its entropy, and that relabelling values one-to-one leaves it unchanged.
  - Its key result is **strong subadditivity**, $H(a,b,c) + H(c) \le H(a,c) + H(b,c)$. The proof is a double-counting bound, $\sum_x n_{ac}(x)\,n_{bc}(x) / (n_{abc}(x)\,n_c(x)) \le N$, combined with $\log t \le t - 1$. Ordinary subadditivity and the data-processing inequality are derived from it.
- **`Catalog/MachineLearning/BatterySynergy/MutualInformation.lean`** defines mutual information as $H(L) + H(f) - H(L,f)$ and derives its basic laws from the entropy library. These are: it is nonnegative, it is at most the label's entropy and at most the statistic's, post-processing a statistic cannot increase it, adding a dial $g$ raises it by at most $H(g)$, and it equals $H(L)$ when the statistic determines the label.

